From e5effb8637d4ff79682e751573e04e4169569f72 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Roger Luethi <luethi@osism.tech>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 09:38:43 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Do not let an unrelated container removal fail a facts lookup

kolla_container_facts with action=get_containers inspects every
container on the host before it applies the name filter, and it inspects
them twice: containers.list() already issues one inspect per container,
and the explicit container.reload() repeats all of them. A container
removed during either pass raises docker.errors.NotFound and aborts the
task, even when the caller asked about a single unrelated service.

In practice that means one short-lived container exiting with --rm can
break a service deploy. Observed in CI on the first task of the rabbitmq
role, where it left RabbitMQ undeployed and the deployment failed
downstream of it.

Filter by name before reloading, so only containers the caller asked
about are inspected a second time; pass ignore_removed=True so a removal
during list() drops that container rather than raising; and catch
NotFound around the reload, since ignore_removed does not reach it. A
container that no longer exists has no facts, so skipping it is the
correct result rather than an error.

The reload is kept rather than dropped. It is redundant on docker, where
containers.list() returns fully inspected objects, but not on podman: on
the libpod path podman-py returns sparse objects, attrs["State"] is a
plain string there, and the State.Health lookup below would raise
AttributeError without the reload.

Signed-off-by: Roger Luethi <luethi@osism.tech>
---
 ansible/library/kolla_container_facts.py | 10 ++++++++--
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/ansible/library/kolla_container_facts.py b/ansible/library/kolla_container_facts.py
index 9886d5252..4690454b1 100644
--- a/ansible/library/kolla_container_facts.py
+++ b/ansible/library/kolla_container_facts.py
@@ -167,12 +167,18 @@ class ContainerFactsWorker():
         get_all_containers = args.get('get_all_containers', False)
         self.result['containers'] = dict()
 
-        containers = self.client.containers.list(all=get_all_containers)
+        containers = self.client.containers.list(all=get_all_containers,
+                                                 ignore_removed=True)
         for container in containers:
-            container.reload()
             container_name = container.name
             if names and container_name not in names:
                 continue
+            try:
+                container.reload()
+            except self.containerError.NotFound:
+                # The container was removed between listing and inspecting
+                # it. It is gone, so it has no facts to report.
+                continue
             # NOTE(r-krcek): For performance reasons don't include
             # healthcheck logs. It can contain MBs worth of data!
             container.attrs["State"].get("Health", dict()).pop("Log", None)
